			"date": "2025-11-12T15:10:41.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://front-end.social/@ppk\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ppk</span></a></span> I also worry about React and Next being the default in LLMs like GPT-5, which means more and more of that code being spat out into the web. I guess the one saving grace for JavaScript is the ECMAScript standard seems to be managed by quite a diverse crowd (e.g. this post by <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://hachyderm.io/@marypcbuk\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>marypcbuk</span></a></span> : <a href=\"https://thenewstack.io/javascript-standards-update-whats-new-in-ecmascript-2025/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">thenewstack.io/javascript-stan</span><span class=\"invisible\">dards-update-whats-new-in-ecmascript-2025/</span></a>)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
