			},
			"date": "2025-11-12T19:18:00.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ricmac\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ricmac</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://hachyderm.io/@marypcbuk\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>marypcbuk</span></a></span> The LLM problem is real, but I see it as an extension of the recruiting advantage React-based companies claim to have. LLMs may make the problem more massive, but not fundamentally different.</p><p>I don't know enough about PHP and Ruby to decide whether Vercel's sponsorship is equivalent to the hold DHH and Wordpress have/had.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
