			"date": "2025-11-18T18:44:19.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://front-end.social/@ppk\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ppk</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ricmac\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ricmac</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I looked at the nextjs ecosystem last year before the first time the Vercel CEO gave customers a reason to leave; there have been more moves since to make nextjs work better other places because, to the original question, there is a whole lot more governance in JavaScript itself and (despite Brendan Eich) a lot fewer patience with problematic people. ie I've seen a LOT of people leaving Vercel because of this and I may be missing something but can't offhand think of other examples of what I see people call 'fashtech' that involve JavaScript. the funding is maybe a bit more secure because of the browsers and Bloomberg, so maybe less opportunity for an Anduril to buy its way in to projects with sponsorship</p><p><a href=\"https://thenewstack.io/opennext-gets-closer-to-making-next-js-truly-portable/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">thenewstack.io/opennext-gets-c</span><span class=\"invisible\">loser-to-making-next-js-truly-portable/</span></a></p>",
